1
00:00:00,028 --> 00:00:04,520
Have you ever wondered how navigation
satellites high above Earth can watch your

2
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:05,320
every step?

3
00:00:05,775 --> 00:00:10,660
The Global Positioning System does not
require your mobile phone to transmit any

4
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:11,460
data.

5
00:00:11,637 --> 00:00:16,740
It operates completely independently of
any mobile telephone network or internet

6
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:17,540
connection.

7
00:00:18,145 --> 00:00:19,760
Even if you turn off mobile data,

8
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:23,940
the system still delivers accurate
location and time information.

9
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:30,100
In reality, your mobile phone functions
purely as a silent and passive radio

10
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:30,900
receiver.

11
00:00:31,482 --> 00:00:36,080
The United States Space Force owns the
satellite system and operates it through

12
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:37,300
Mission Delta 31.

13
00:00:38,161 --> 00:00:41,140
Although the United States government
maintains the constellation,

14
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:44,840
anyone with a receiver
can freely access it.

15
00:00:45,397 --> 00:00:50,220
The system was originally created for
military use before being opened to civilians

16
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:51,420
across the globe.

17
00:00:52,103 --> 00:00:57,580
Civilian access was authorised by President
Ronald Reagan after an airliner was

18
00:00:57,580 --> 00:01:00,020
tragically shot down in 1983.

19
00:01:00,978 --> 00:01:06,280
Early civilian signals were intentionally
degraded by a policy known as Selective

20
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:07,080
Availability.

21
00:01:07,753 --> 00:01:12,640
This deliberate error degraded civilian
accuracy to roughly 100 metres

22
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:15,380
until the policy was ended in 2000.

23
00:01:16,485 --> 00:01:21,118
Today, modern consumer smartphones
can determine a position to within 4.9

24
00:01:21,118 --> 00:01:23,460
metres or even better.

25
00:01:25,184 --> 00:01:27,580
To understand how your
device knows its place,

26
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:29,220
we must look up into orbit.

27
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:35,520
The space segment of GPS consists
of between 24 and 32 operational

28
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:36,320
satellites.

29
00:01:37,056 --> 00:01:41,520
These spacecraft circle our planet in
what engineers call medium Earth orbit.

30
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:47,340
Each satellite travels at an altitude
of approximately 20,200 kilometres

31
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:49,300
above the surface of the Earth.

32
00:01:50,010 --> 00:01:55,080
The satellites are arranged across 6 separate
orbital planes tilted at approximately

33
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:57,280
55 degrees to the equator.

34
00:01:57,929 --> 00:02:01,680
Each orbit takes approximately
11 hours and 58 minutes,

35
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:05,120
completing 2 full trips
every sidereal day.

36
00:02:05,828 --> 00:02:11,000
The satellites are not spaced at equal
90-degree intervals along each orbital ring.

37
00:02:11,710 --> 00:02:14,500
Instead, their spacing
varies between 30 degrees,

38
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:18,740
105 degrees, and 120 degrees apart.

39
00:02:19,587 --> 00:02:24,960
This deliberate geometry guarantees that
at least 6 satellites remain visible from

40
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:26,280
almost anywhere on Earth.

41
00:02:27,021 --> 00:02:32,280
Day and night, every satellite continually
broadcasts radio carrier waves down

42
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:33,340
toward the ground below.

43
00:02:34,136 --> 00:02:39,500
The transmission contains a unique
pseudorandom code made of ones and zeros that

44
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:40,933
receivers recognise.

45
00:02:41,376 --> 00:02:46,260
It also broadcasts a continuous data
message containing its exact orbital

46
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:48,600
position and the departure time.

47
00:02:49,239 --> 00:02:54,500
This detailed orbital data is called an
ephemeris and allows receivers to pinpoint

48
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:55,300
the satellite.
